1,0$ source : http://www.svims.ca/council/Ramar2.htm /// A microscopic key to Ramaria species known from The Pacific Northwestern United States.
1,cs15$Bibliographie
1,0$
µm

1,2 $ A. Basidiocarps lignicolous or humicolous, small to medium sized, often dingy colored; rhizomorphs often present and binding substrate, of monomitic to dimitic construction; spores warted to echinate, never smooth; clamp connections present, often conspicuously inflated in the rhizomorphic strands
1,3 $ B. Basidiocarps terricolous, medium sized to large, often brightly colored; rhizomorphs lacking or if present of monomitic construction; spores smooth, warted or striate, not echinate; clamp connections either lacking or not conspicuously inflated
2,cs2 $ A. Spores echinulate; basidiocarps humicolous; rhizomorphs monomitic $ subgenus Echinoramaria$s" subgenus Echinoramaria"
2,cs3 $ B. Spores smooth or warted; basidiocarps humicolous or lignicolous; rhizomorphs dimitic in most species (monomitic in R. apiculata and R. suecica) $ subgenus Lentoramaria$s"subgenus Lentoramaria"
3,cs4 $ A. Spores striate, often >11 µm long; branches mostly white to cream colored or cream colored with brightly colored apices; stipe context generally amyloid (clamp connections always present; stipe single, often massive) $ subgenus Ramaria$s"subgenus Ramaria"
3,4 $ B. Spores smooth or warted, generally <11 µm long; branches and apices mostly brightly colored; stipe context mostly non-amyloid (clamp connections either present or lacking; stipe single (then usually slender), fasciculate or compound, small or medium sized)
4,cs5 $ A. Clamp connections present $ subgenus Laeticolora, species with clamped basidia$s"subgenus Laeticolora, species with clamped basidia"
4,cs6 $ B. Clamp connections absent $ subgenus Laeticolora, species without clamped basidia$s"subgenus Laeticolora, species without clamped basidia"


cs2,0$subgenus Echinoramaria

1,2 $ A. Spore Lm < 5.0 µm, spines generally <0.3 µm
1,3 $ B. Spore Lm > 5.5 µm, spines mostly >0.5 µm
2,0 $ A. Branches open, delicate, chamois to honey yellow; spore spines up to 0.3 µm, Lm = 4.8 µm (4.4-5.7 × 2.6-3.5 µm); under conifers $ R. myceliosa
2,0 $ B. Branches congested, irregular cream buff to yellow-ochre; spore spines fine to verrucose, Lm = 4.4 µm, (4.2-5.2 × 2.8-3.5 µm); under Pinus $ R. curta
3,4 $ A. Basidiocarp bruising blue green upon collecting; spore Lm = >= 8.2 µm 
3,5 $ B. Basidiocarp not bruising blue green upon collecting or if blue-green stains present, inconspicuous and limited to small areas on stipe (R. mutabilis); spore Lm <= 7.8 µm
4,0 $ A. Spore Lm = 9.5 µm (8.2-11.1 × 4.4-5.5 µm, spines 0.5-0.7 µm); basidiocarp bulky (up to 15 cm tall); major branches lobed in cross-section $ R. glaucoaromatica
4,0 $ B. Spore Lm = 8.2 µm (7.0-9.0 × 3.7-4.5 µm, spines up to 1.0 µm); basidiocarp diminutive (usually < 5 cm tall); branches often flattened $ R. abietina
5,6 $ A. Spore Lm <= 6.5 µm (length range 4.5-8.0 µm)
5,7 $ B. Spore Lm >= 7.4 µm (length range 6-10 µm)
6,0 $ A. Small areas of stipe often with blue-green stains; dried branch tips olive-brown; spore spines <= 0.6 µm, Lm = 6.53 µm (5.5-7.5 × 3.3-4.1 µm) $ R. mutabilis
6,0 $ B. Stipe white bruising brown, lacking blue-green stains; dried branch tips white; spore spines longer, <= 1.2 µm, Lm = 6.28 µm (4.5-8.0 × 3.0-4.5 µm) $ R. argentea
7,0 $ A. Branches completely fertile (e.g., hymenium amphigenous); rhizomorphs yellowish white to pale yellow; spore Lm = 7.8 µm, spines up to 1.0 µm (6.3-10 × 3.3-4.8 µm) $ R. eumorpha  
7,8$ B. Branches with significant decurrent sterile patches (e.g., hymenium unilateral); rhizomorphs white; spore Lm ~7.4 µm, spines shorter, less than 0.8 µm8
8,0 $ A. Spore spines up to 0.8 µm (6.5-8.9 × 3.5-5.4 µm, Lm = 7.38 µm); basidiocarps slender and weak with one or more branches often splitting away from stipe or bending to touch the substrate; stipe not staining or bruising; branch tips tan to golden $ R. flaccida
8,0 $ B. Spore spines longer, up to 2.0 µm (6.0-8.6 × 3.3-4.5 µm, Lm = 7.45 µm); basidiocarps stout; stipe browning when handled; branch tips honey-brown to whitish $ R. incognita



cs3,0$   subgenus Lentoramaria

1,0 $ A. Spores smooth under 1000x $ Lentaria or Clavicorona (not covered in these keys.)
1,2 $ B. Spores ornamented
2,0 $ A. Spores average ~ 6.0 µm long; basidiocarp mostly off-white to pale ochraceous (humicolous; rhizomorphs dimitic; spores 5.5-7.1 × 3.3-4.4 µm, Lm = 6.0 µm) $ R. gracilis
2,3 $ B. Spores average >= 7.0 µm long; basidiocarp variously colored (humicolous or lignicolous; rhizomorphs mono- or dimitic)
3,4 $ A. Spores average <= 7.5 µm long; young branches pinkish buff to ruddy purplish with white to pale cream tips; lignicolous
3,5 $ B. Spores average > 8.0 µm long; young branches and tips variously colored; humicolous or lignicolous
4,0 $ A. Rhizomorphic strands turning bright mauve pink in 10% KOH; hymenium amphigenous or, if not, with sterile areas running down from axils in narrow lines; stipe grey to brownish; branches dull violaceous to pinkish; spore Lm ~ 7.5 µm (6.3-9.5 × 4.1-5.5 µm) $ R. rubella f. rubella
4,0 $ B. Rhizomorphic strands unchanging or yellowish in 10% KOH; hymenium clearly unilateral (especially in dried specimens); stipe whitish; branches pinkish buff; spore Lm ~ 7.1 µm (6.3-8.1 × 4.4-5.9 µm) $ R. rubella f. blanda
5,6 $ A. Basidiocarps humicolous
5,7 $ B. Basidiocarps lignicolous
6,0 $ A. Spore Wm = ~ 4.3 µm; rhizomorphs monomitic and with unornamented inflated clamps; spore Lm ~ 9.0 µm (8.1-10.4 × 3.7-5.2 µm) $ R. suecica
6,0 $ B. Spore Wm = ~ 5.0 µm; rhizomorphs dimitic and with conspicuously ornamented inflated clamps; spore Lm ~ 9.5 µm (8.1-11.1 × 4.4-5.9 µm) $ R. rainierensis
7,0 $ A. Upper branches and apices light to citron yellow; spore Lm~ 8.4 µm (7.5-10 × 4-5 µm) $ R. stricta
7,8 $ B. Upper branches and apices dull ochraceous, dull buffy tan to cream colored; spores various
8,9 $ A. Rhizomorphs monomitic; Lm ~ 9.7 µm
8,11 $ B. Rhizomorphs dimitic; Lm <= 8.5 µm (R. tsugina Lm = 9.1 µm)
9,0 $ A. Upper branches and apices with light green to light bluish green colorations; spore Lm ~ 9.7 µm (8.5-11.0 × 4.1-5.2 µm) $ R. apiculata var. apiculata
9,10 $ B. Upper branches and apices without greenish colorations
10,0 $ A. Basidiocarps small; branches sparse ascending to erect, not crowded, not anastomosing; lignicolous; spore Lm = 9.79 µm (9.2-11.0 × 3.8-5.0 µm) $ R. apiculata var. brunnea
10,0 $ B. Basidiocarps usually large and broadly ovoid in outline; branches numerous, congested and often anastomosing; on wood debris or sawdust; spores similar to R. apiculata var. brunnea $ R. apiculata var. brunnea f. compacta
11,0 $ A. Stipe, branches or apices with green stains; spore Lm ~ 9.1 µm (7-9.3 × 3.5-4.2 µm) $ R. tsugina
11,0 $ B. Stipe, branches and apices lacking green stains; spore Lm ~ 8.1 µm (7.8-10 × 3.7-4.8 µm) $ R. concolor

