cs4,0$subgenus Ramaria

1,2 $ A. Entire basidiocarp white to cream colored, sometimes with faint violet tinged apices
1,3 $ B. Basidiocarp distinctly salmon, pink or red colored and with brightly colored apices
2,0 $ A. Spores Lm = 14.1 µm (12-18 × 3.5-6 µm), rarely less than 13 µm $ R. subviolacea
2,0 $ B. Spores Lm = 11.8 µm (10.4-13.7 × 4.0-5.5 µm), rarely > 12.5 µm $ R. rubrievanescens
3,4 $ A. Spores Lm <= 12.2 µm
3,5 $ B. Spores Lm >= 13.5 µm
4,0 $ A. Stipe milk-white (discoloring yellowish), bruising brownish violet; apices buffy pink to pale rose when young, fading soon after collecting or during maturation to yellowish white; autumnal; spores Lm = 11.8 µm (10.4-13.7 × 4.0-5.5 µm) $ R. rubrievanescens
4,0 $ B. Stipe white to yellowish-white, surface not staining or bruising; apices pale pink to buffy or blood red, fading over time to dull rosy pink, color persisting after collecting; autumnal or vernal; spores Lm = 12.2 µm, (10.4-10.4-15.5 × 4.0-5.0 µm) $ R. rubripermanens
5,0 $ A. Terminal branches red to pinkish red; spores Lm = 13.8 × 4.7 µm (11-17 × 4-6 µm) $ R. botrytis var. botrytis
5,0 $ B. Terminal branches light orange to orange-brown; spores slightly shorter than above: Lm = 13.5 × 4.7 µm (12-16 × 4-6 µm) $ R. botrytis var. aurantiiramosa


cs5,0$subgenus Laeticolora, species with clamped basidia

1,2 $ A. Basidiocarps (at least at the stipe apex or lower branches) lilac, violet or purple
1,3 $ B. Basidiocarp not lilac, violet or purple
2,0 $ A. Branches and apices intensely violet to purple (amethyst-lilac when young, remaining so or aging to ochraceous purple; spores Lm = 10.29 µm (9-11.2 × 4.7-5.4 µm) $ R. purpurissima var. purpurissima
2,0 $ B. Branches and apices less intense (pale to dull violet when young, aging smoky drab, cinnamon, or dark olive); spores Lm = 10.42 µm (9-13 × 4.3-5.4 µm) $ R. violaceibrunnea
3,0 $ A. Stipe compound (a gelatinous mass of fused stipes); spores Lm = 8.9 µm (7-10 × 4.5-6 µm) $ R. gelatinosa var. oregonensis
3,4 $ B. Stipe various (not a mass of fused gelatinous stipes) or spores not as above
4,0 $ A. Any basidiocarp part bruising brown or wine-colored immediately when cut; FSW instantly turning stipe context blue-green; white stipe mostly single and covered with white tomentum; branches "maize yellow" when young, then red-brown with tips remaining yellow; spores Lm = 11.76 µm (9.5-14 × 4.2-6.4 µm) $ R. testaceoflava
4,5 $ B. Not as above
5,6 $ A. Stipe with 'rusty root' (brown band) in radial section; stipe flesh usually blue green in FSW; stipe base often streaked with red-brown superficial hyphae
5,8 $ B. Stipe lacking 'rusty root' (brown band) in radial section; stipe flesh not blue green in FSW; stipe usually lacking streaked red-brown superficial hyphae
6,7 $ A. Stipe flesh instantly turning blue-green on application of FSW
6,0 $ B. Stipe flesh not turning blue-green on application of FSW $ undescribed Ramaria spp.Petersen & Scates (1988) knew of two undescribed taxa with brownish stipe flesh that did not react immediately to ferric salts. Both were clamped and autumnal fruiters.
7,0 $ A. Stipe context amyloid (dried specimens instantly turning dark brown); branches light orange to salmon; spores Lm = 8.9 µm (7-10 × 3-4 µm) $ R. amyloidea
7,0 $ B. Stipe context non-amyloid; branches (creamy) white to pale yellow; spores Lm = 9.0 µm (8-12 × 3.5-5 µm) $ R. velocimutans
8,0 $ A. Stipe slender, sub-fasciculate, covered with a well developed white tomentum; branches and apices (citron) yellow to pale salmon; acantho-dendroid gloeoplerous hyphae (multi-directional, freely branched, studded with lateral spurs, narrow, thin-walled, in cotton blue densely cyanophilous granular) present in the peripheral stipe context $ R. cystidiophora (see key to varieties)
8,cs7 $key to varieties or cystidiophora

8,9 $ B. Not as above; acantho-dendroid gloeoplerous hyphae absent in peripheral context of stipe
9,10 $ A. Base and lower stipe with wine-colored stains
9,11 $ B. Base lacking wine-colored stains or bruises
10,0 $ A. Branches peach to salmon with yellow tips; spores Lm = 10.2 µm (9-11 × 4-5 µm) $ R. maculatipes
10,0 $ B. Both branches and tips yellow-white to pale yellow; spores Lm = 12.51 µm (11.2-14.0 × 4.3-5.0 µm) $ R. vinosimaculans
11,12 $ A. Spores Lm >= 12.5 µm (warted); stipe flesh non-amyloid
11,14 $ B. Spores Lm <= 12 µm (smooth to warted); stipe flesh amyloid or non-amyloid
12,0 $ A. Basidiocarp white to pale yellow; stipe massive; vernal (spores Lm = 13.28 µm, 11.6-15.8 × 4.0-5.0 µm) $ R. thiersii
12,13 $ B. Basidiocarp orange to salmon; stipe slender; autumnal
13,0 $ A. Both branches and tips intense orange; stipe broadly conical with small abortive branchlets frequent on the upper base; spores Wm = 4.5 µm, Lm = 13.4 µm (11-15 × 3-5 µm) $ R. largentii
13,0 $ B. Branches flesh-pink to salmon colored with bright orange tips; stipe bluntly acute or obconical and lacking abortive branchlets; spores Wm = 5.3 µm, Lm = 13.7 µm (12.6-16.3 × 4.8-6.3 µm) $ R. distinctissima var. americana
14,15 $ A. Branches orange, salmon, or red; if yellow, then branch context salmon or orange
14,18 $ B. Branches yellow to cream colored (lacking orange to red to salmon colors)
15,0 $ A. Spores Wm = 5.4 µm; branches peach, light red to salmon colored & stipe context non-amyloid; apices yellow when young; basidiocarp staining or bruising wherever handled; spores coarsely warted (Lm = 10.4 µm; 9-12 × 4.5-6 µm) $ R. formosa
15,16 $ B. Spores Wm <= 4.5; branches orange (if salmon colored, stipe context amyloid, see R. rubricarnata); apices yellow to orange; basidiocarp browning or not where handled; spores ornamented with fine to low warts and ridges
16,17 $ A. Stipe base flesh non-amyloid; stipe single to fasciculate, slender to large; branches orange to light red with concolorous or yellow tips; autumnal
16,0 $ B. Stipe base flesh amyloid; stipe single, large to massive; branches light orange, pale salmon-buff to yellow to salmon-orange with yellow tips; autumn & spring $ R. rubricarnata
16,cs9,0$ varities of  rubricarnata

17,0 $ A. Spores Lm = 10.6 µm (8-13 × 3-5 µm); basidiocarp elongated; branches light orange to light red with sunflower/dark yellow or chrome orange tips; stipe context fleshy fibrous; bruising or staining reactions slight or entirely absent $ R. leptoformosa
17,cs8 $ B. Spores Lm = 8.1-8.6 µm (6.5-10 µm); basidiocarp mostly compact; branches pale to deep orange with orange or yellow tips; stipe context subgelatinous to rubbery; outer stipe occasionally with dull violet bruised areas $ R. sandaracina (see key to varieties)
