1,2 $ Spore Lm = 8.0 µm; branches yellow or salmon
1,3 $ Spore Lm >= 9.5 µm; branches yellow, lacking salmon tinge or pigments
2,0 $ Branches and tips yellow to citron yellow; spores Lm = 8.0 µm (7-9 × 3-4 µm) $ R. cystidiophora var. cystidiophora
2,0 $ Branches pale salmon with light clear yellow young tips (tips faded when mature); spores Lm = 8.2 µm (7.6-8.6 × 3.2-3.9 µm) $ R. cystidiophora var. anisata
3,0 $ Stipe context cartilagino-gelatinous (basal hyphal walls gelatinizing); basidiocarps not bruising or staining; odor fabaceous; spores Lm = 9.7 µm (8-11 × 3.5-5 µm) $ R. cystidiophora var. fabiolens
3,4 $ Stipe context fleshy-fibrous (no gelatinization); basidiocarps bruising brown to reddish; odor sweet or none; spores Lm >= 10.3 µm
4,0 $ Spores Lm = 10.3 µm (9-13 × 3.5-5.0 µm); basidiocarps bruising brown $ R. cystidiophora var. citronella
4,0 $ Spores Lm = 11.8 µm (10-14 × 3.5-5.0 µm); basidiocarps bruising reddish-brown $ R. cystidiophora var. maculans



cs8,0$ sandaracina (see key to varieties)

1,0 $ Apices bright yellow when young; spores Lm = 8.6 µm (6.5-9.0 × 3.5-4.5 µm) $ R. sandaracina var. euosma
1,2 $ Apices orange; spore Lm = 8.1-8.3 µm
2,0 $ Basidiocarps broad (commonly > 8 cm wide); stipe base subgelatinous with gelatinous streaks present when cut and numerous elongated primary branches arising from a broad fasciculate to compound base; branches and tips salmon to orange; spores Lm = 8.3 µm (7-10 × 3.5-5 µm) $ R. sandaracina var. chondrobasis
2,0 $ Basidiocarps slender (usually < 8 cm wide); stipe base sometimes slightly gelatinous in part with several primary branches arising from a single stipe; lower branches and upper base bright yellow, upper branches and tips deep orange; spores Lm = 8.1µm (6.5-9.0 × 3.5-4.5µm) $ R. sandaracina var. sandaracina


cs9,0$varities of  rubricarnata  Key to R. rubricarnata varieties (from Petersen & Scates 1988):

1,0 $ Autumnal; spores Lm = ~10 µm (8.6-1.2 × 4.0-4.7 µm); branches pale cream to salmon-yellow (occasionally yellow) with pale to light yellow tips $ R. rubricarnata var. rubricarnata
1,2 $ Vernal; spores Lm >= 11.1 µm; stipe,branches, and tips as above or paler
2,0 $ Branches short stalked, salmon-orange to light salmon with salmon-orange flesh and yellow to rich yellow tips; spores Lm = 11.1 µm (10.4-12.2 × 4.0-5.0 µm) $ R. rubricarnata var. verna
2,0 $ Branches elongated, buff colored to pale buffy yellow with muted pinkish-buff branch flesh and dull greenish-yellow (young) to light yellow (mature) tips; spores Lm = 11.4 µm (9.7-14.4 × 4.0-4.7 µm) $ R. rubricarnata var. pallida


cs10,0$Key to R. conjunctipes varieties:
1,0 $ Base a fascicle of stringy, white stipes; sparsely branched above; fruiting bodies rarely taller than 10 cm $ R. conjunctipes var. sparsiramosa
1,0 $ Base single to densely fasciculate; stipes not stringy; densely branched above; fruiting bodies up to 18 cm tall $ R. conjunctipes var. tsugensis


cs11,0$Key to R. flavigelatinosa varieties:

1,2 $ Basidiocarp predominately yellow with orange or salmon colors in the upper branches due to the salmon-colored branch context
1,3 $ Basidiocarp entirely yellow; branch context yellow
2,0 $ Spores average = 9.6 × 4.1 µm (8-11 × 3.5-4.5 µm); stipe context firmly gelatinous (translucent white) when fresh $ R. flavigelatinosa var. carnisalmonea
2,0 $ Spores average = 12 × 4.5 µm (9-15 × 4-6 µm); stipe context cartilaginous when fresh $ R. flavigelatinosa var. megalospora
3,0 $ Spores average = 9.6 × 4.1 µm (8-11 × 3.5-6 µm); odor fabaceous or not distinctive; stipe context firmly gelatinous (translucent white) when fresh $ R. flavigelatinosa var. flavigelatinosa
3,0 $ Spores average = 10 × 4.8 µm (8.5-13 × 3.5-6 µm); odor sweet; stipe context cartilaginous when fresh $ R. flavigelatinosa var. fragrans

cs12,0$Key to R. cyaneigranosa varieties
1,0 $ Apices concolorous with branches, never yellow; internodes and general habit slender and conspicuously elongated; spore Lm = 9.2 µm (8-10 × 4-5 µm) $ R. cyaneigranosa var. elongata
1,2 $ Apices dotted with yellow; internodes and general habit not conspicuously elongated; spore Lm >= 9.6 µm
2,0 $ Branches light red; base single or sub-compound, thick or slightly bulbous; spore Lm = 11 µm (8.5-15 × 4-6 µm) $ R. cyaneigranosa var. cyaneigranosa
2,0 $ Branches salmon to peach; base single, steeply tapered, slender (covered by a white basal tomentum); spore Lm = 9.6 µm (7-11 × 3.5-6 µm) $ R. cyaneigranosa var. persicina


cs13,0$Key to forms of R. concolor
1,0 $ Branches open, lax, curved ascending $ R. concolor f. marrii
1,2 $ Branches erect, often crowded but not lax and open
2,0 $ Branch axils with greenish colors $ R. concolor f. tsugina [R. tsugina]
2,3 $ Branch axils concolorous with branches, without greenish colorations
3,0 $ Basidiocarp base, stipe and lower branches deep chocolate brown $ R. concolor f. fumida
3,0 $ Basidiocarp base and stipe more or less concolorous with branches, ochraceous brown to deep cinnamon brown but not deep chocolate brown $ R. concolor f. concolor


